{-
   The host array.

   **`.js.yana`, so the file itself is the platform** - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5. On a native build
   nothing here is read at all, which is what makes a host operation unreachable rather than merely
   unused there. The alternative is twenty-five `@platform(js)` attributes saying the same thing
   twenty-five times; the name says it once, and there is no way to write a declaration here that
   forgets to.

   `%a` is the array, and what a `%a` means on this target is in host.h: an opaque reference to
   storage, since nothing this target can express does arithmetic on one. That is why these need no
   type of their own. A `HostArray(a)` newtype would type exactly the same programs and would put a
   host element in a *record* place rather than a pointer one - which moves every element of every
   container inside the ownership graph and makes a write through one owe a drop the native path
   does not owe.
-}

{-
   The host, as five shapes rather than as a list of its methods.

   **The compiler holds no host name.** It used to hold twenty-seven of them - `charCodeAt`,
   `copyWithin`, `Date.now`, `getUint32` - each as a C++ emitter matched to a declaration below by
   its spelling, so reaching a twenty-eighth was a compiler change. Analysis-JS.md §2.4 rules host
   knowledge out of code generation; it had only moved up a stage.

   So the name is an *argument*. Each of these is one shape the emitted source has - a field, a call
   of n arguments on a receiver, a call on the host's global scope, a binary operator, a `throw` -
   and the member's spelling is a string literal the compiler reads at the call and writes into the
   output. Everything below is written over them, and a host method nobody has needed yet is one
   line of Yana rather than a row in a table somewhere else.

   **It costs nothing.** The wrappers are `@inline(always)`, so a call to one is replaced by its body
   where it is written, with no optimizer having run; and a string literal on this target is a
   constant with no storage and no global behind it, so the name is spent at compile time and leaves
   nothing in the program. Both halves are load-bearing: without the first these would be real calls
   in every unoptimized build, and without the second the literal would be a value in every block.

   **One role per arity**, because a declaration has one arity and there is no variadic form to
   write - the same reason `syscall0` through `syscall6` are seven declarations. Three arguments is
   as far as anything here reaches; a fourth is a row in `op.def` and a line here.

   The subject is a bare type variable, so what a receiver *is* is each caller's business: a `%a` for
   an array, a `String` for text, a `Double` for `toExponential`. The compiler prints what arrived.
-}
@builtin(hostField) pub fn hostField(self: a, member: String) -> b
@builtin(hostCall0) pub fn hostCall0(self: a, member: String) -> b
@builtin(hostCall1) pub fn hostCall1(self: a, member: String, arg0: b) -> c
@builtin(hostCall2) pub fn hostCall2(self: a, member: String, arg0: b, arg1: c) -> d
@builtin(hostCall3) pub fn hostCall3(self: a, member: String, arg0: b, arg1: c, arg2: d) -> e

{-
   `Path.of.a.name(args)` - a call on something in the host's global scope.

   Genuinely a different shape from `hostCall`, rather than a receiver that happens to be a name:
   there is no receiver among the arguments at all, because the receiver is written literally into
   the emitted source. The whole dotted path is the member name.
-}
@builtin(hostGlobal0) pub fn hostGlobal0(path: String) -> a
@builtin(hostGlobal1) pub fn hostGlobal1(path: String, arg0: a) -> b

{-
   `lhs <operator> rhs`, whose "member name" is the operator's own spelling.

   Needed because a host string's comparison is an operator and not a method: `s.concat(t)` exists
   and would have fitted `hostCall1`, but there is no method for `<` at all - `localeCompare` is
   locale-sensitive, which is exactly the cross-engine disagreement Implementation-String.md part 6
   refuses. The host's own `<` on two strings is already the raw unit-wise order part 3 specifies.
-}
@builtin(hostBinary) pub fn hostBinary(lhs: a, operator: String, rhs: b) -> c

{-
   `throw message` - how a program stops on this target.

   A statement rather than a call, which is why it is a shape of its own: JavaScript has no `abort`,
   and the one thing every host agrees on is that an exception nobody catches ends the program with
   the value it carried reported. The message is thrown as it stands rather than wrapped in an
   `Error`, because what it has to do is say which check failed.

   The text is *here* rather than in the backend, and that is the whole of what the literal
   parameter bought: an emitter that wrote the message itself was a code generator holding a string a
   reader would look for in `lib`.
-}
@builtin(hostThrow) pub fn hostThrow(message: String) -> {}

{-
   `DataView.getUint32(index, littleEndian)` - a machine word out of a byte array, and back into one.

   Two shapes of their own rather than `hostCall`s, because the receiver in the emitted source is
   *not* the argument: a `DataView` has to exist over the array's buffer, and building one per call
   is fifteen times slower than the shift chain this replaced - so the emitter hangs one off the
   array on first use and every later word rides on it. The name is still the accessor's, which is
   where the width lives.
-}
@builtin(hostViewRead) pub fn hostViewRead(self: %U8, accessor: String, index: Size, little: Bool) -> a
@builtin(hostViewWrite) pub fn hostViewWrite(self: %U8, accessor: String, index: Size, value: a, little: Bool) -> {}

-- An empty host array - `[]`. The array literal builds its own with the elements in it, since a
-- literal is one node rather than a call plus n pushes; this is what `emptyArray` is.
@builtin(hostArray) pub fn hostArray() -> %a

{-
   There is deliberately no `hostPush`, and the reason is ownership rather than taste.

   A host operation's arguments are *uses*: the ownership passes read a move out of an `InstMove` or
   out of an assignment into a place, and an operand of an `InstNative` is neither. So
   `self.push(item)` appends the value and leaves this frame still owning it, and the frame releases
   it at the end of the block - an element the array is holding, released while it holds it. It was
   written, and it read as if it worked, and the counter in `HostArray.yana` is what caught it.

   Appending is `arr[arr.length] = item` instead, which is `hostWrite` below and is an assignment
   through a place. That is the *same* hand-over the native `store(self.run.items + count, item)` is,
   read by the same pass, which is the property worth having: a container's two bodies differ in how
   an element is reached and in nothing about who owns it.
-}

-- `self.length`. A `Size`, which on this target is `Int` - and a host array's length is a `uint32`
-- by specification, so there is nothing wider to describe (§4.4).
@inline(always) pub fn hostLength(self: %a) -> Size = hostField(self, "length")

{-
   Closing a gap - `self.copyWithin(to, from, end)`, the move without the shortening.

   The counterpart of the native build's `copyMemory`, and it is deliberately the *only* one here.
   A `.splice` is not declared beside it. It shortens as well as moves, which is what a container
   wants when the slot at the end has to stop existing, and no container here needs that asked for
   separately: `Array(a)`'s count is `@host` on the row whose elements are host objects,
   so writing the new count *is* the truncation and the duplicate this leaves behind is released by
   the statement that records the removal (see `remove` in `Core/Array.yana`). On the typed row the
   elements are numbers, the stale slot is as inert as the bytes `copyMemory` leaves, and the stored
   count is what says how many are live.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostCopyWithin(self: %a, to: Size, from: Size, end: Size) -> {}
    = hostCall3(self, "copyWithin", to, from, end) :: {}

{-
   Whether this element's array has a capacity that has to be managed - see typedArrayFor.

   A compile-time constant with an argument, and the argument is what carries the question: an
   intrinsic is handed its result type rather than its type arguments, so the element is read off the
   array reference it is asked about. `opt_fold` then removes the branch it guards, in both
   directions, which is what lets `reserve` be one body over two representations rather than a
   `@platform` split that this target cannot spell - the row is chosen by the *element*, and
   `@platform` chooses by target.
-}
@builtin(hostFixedCapacity) pub fn hostFixedCapacity(self: %a) -> Bool

{-
   A new array of `capacity` elements with this one's contents copied into it.

   Only ever reached where `hostFixedCapacity` said yes, so it is the typed row's growth and nothing
   else - a host array grows by being written past its end and never calls this.
-}
@builtin(hostGrow) pub fn hostGrow(self: %a, capacity: Size) -> %a

{-
   The element - `self[index]`, in the four positions an element is reached from.

   `hostRead` answers the element *by value*, which is what makes `let ->doomed = hostRead(xs, i)` a
   move out of the array: it is the same line `*(self.run.items + i)` is on the native side, and it
   is what a container's teardown and its `remove` are written with.

   `hostAt` and `hostAtMut` answer a borrow, and carry the `return` marker for the reason `borrow`
   does: the result names storage this function was handed rather than storage of its own.

   `hostWrite` declares `->value` for the reason `store` does on the other target: what the array
   holds afterwards *is* that value, so the frame that owned it stops owing it a drop - and a
   declaration is all an intrinsic has to say so with. `Array.js.yana`'s `push` is where it matters.
-}
@builtin(hostRead) pub fn hostRead(self: %a, index: Size) -> a
@builtin(hostWrite) pub fn hostWrite(self: %a, index: Size, ->value: a) -> {}
@builtin(hostAt) pub fn hostAt(return self: %a, index: Size) -> 'a
@builtin(hostAtMut) pub fn hostAtMut(return self: %a, index: Size) -> &a

{-
   A whole vector out of a host array, and back into one - this target's half of
   Implementation-Vector.md §12's "a way to read a vector out of a `Flat(a)`".

   There is no memory here and so nothing to load: a vector on this target *is* `lanes` values
   (Design-Vector §7.2), so a vector read is `lanes` element reads and a vector write is `lanes`
   element writes. Neither is a loop - the lane count is known where the call is expanded, so each of
   these is straight-line code of a length the type decided.

   `hostVectorUpTo` is the tail, and it is where the two targets genuinely differ. Natively the tail
   reads past the end of the object and the guarantee in §8 says that is safe; here there is no
   guarantee to have, and a read past the end of a host array is `undefined` rather than an
   unspecified byte - so the index of every lane is held at `limit`, which is the last element that
   exists. The lanes past the end therefore repeat the last element, which is a value the caller is
   about to mask off and never a value that is not a number.
-}
@builtin(hostVector) pub fn hostVector(self: %a, index: Size) -> Vec(a)
@builtin(hostVectorUpTo) pub fn hostVectorUpTo(self: %a, index: Size, limit: Size) -> Vec(a)
@builtin(hostSetVector) pub fn hostSetVector(self: %a, index: Size, value: Vec(a)) -> {}

{-
   A machine word out of a byte array, and back into one - this target's half of `readBigU32` and
   its eleven siblings in `Core/Container.yana`.

   `DataView` is the host primitive for exactly this question, and it is the whole reason these are
   intrinsics rather than the shift chains they replace. At 16 and 32 bits the two forms measure the
   same on Node 24 - V8 compiles four subscripts and three shifts about as well as one `getUint32` -
   and at **64 bits it is 2x on a read and 7x on a write**, because a `U64` here is a `bigint` and the
   alternative is building one out of eight bytes.

   **`%U8` and not `%a`.** A `DataView` needs the array's buffer, and only the typed row has one -
   see `typedArrayFor`, where a host array of `U8` is a `Uint8Array` and a host array of anything
   without a typed form is a plain array with no `.buffer` at all. Every caller reads bytes, so
   nothing is given up by saying so in the signature.

   **The order is an argument** rather than six more declarations, because that is where the host
   takes it: `getUint32(index, littleEndian)`. Measured, the flag costs nothing a per-order helper
   would have saved, and it halves the emitted helper count.

   The index is in *bytes* from the start of the array, which is what a caller reading a binary
   format already has and is the one unit a `DataView` deals in.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostReadU16(self: %U8, index: Size, little: Bool) -> U16
    = hostViewRead(self, "getUint16", index, little)
@inline(always) pub fn hostReadU32(self: %U8, index: Size, little: Bool) -> U32
    = hostViewRead(self, "getUint32", index, little)
@inline(always) pub fn hostReadU64(self: %U8, index: Size, little: Bool) -> U64
    = hostViewRead(self, "getBigUint64", index, little)
@inline(always) pub fn hostWriteU16(self: %U8, index: Size, value: U16, little: Bool) -> {}
    = hostViewWrite(self, "setUint16", index, value, little)
@inline(always) pub fn hostWriteU32(self: %U8, index: Size, value: U32, little: Bool) -> {}
    = hostViewWrite(self, "setUint32", index, value, little)
@inline(always) pub fn hostWriteU64(self: %U8, index: Size, value: U64, little: Bool) -> {}
    = hostViewWrite(self, "setBigUint64", index, value, little)

{-
   The host string - Implementation-String.md part 2's JS column.

   `String` here is the host `string` primitive with no wrapper at all, which is what part 2 asks for
   and what makes a Yana string free to hand to any host API. So every operation below is the host's
   own, and none of them allocates anything Yana has to account for: the collector owns every string
   these produce, which is why `Reclaim(String)` has no JS instance.

   Every one of them is a `HostMember` entry in resolve/host.cpp and a declaration here, and that is
   the whole of what adding one costs. **None of them answers a `Bool`**, which is a rule rather than
   a coincidence: a host predicate hands back a host boolean and a `Bool` on this target is the number
   0 or 1 (`boolNumber` in codegen/js/type.cpp), and the `HostCall` arm does not convert. So
   `startsWith` and `endsWith` are *not* here even though the host has both - `Core/Text.js.yana`
   spells them as a `hostSubstring` and a `hostStringEq`, which is one comparison on a short string
   and cannot go wrong in a value position.
-}

-- `self.length`, in UTF-16 code units, which part 3 is explicit is *not* the same number the native
-- build answers for the same content. Both are O(1), and that is the property required to be
-- uniform rather than the value.
@inline(always) pub fn hostStringLength(self: String) -> Size = hostField(self, "length")

-- `self.charCodeAt(index)` - one raw UTF-16 unit, with no decode and no validation that it is a
-- whole code point. The exact counterpart of the native build's byte read.
@inline(always) pub fn hostCharCodeAt(self: String, index: Size) -> Int
    = hostCall1(self, "charCodeAt", index)

{-
   `self.indexOf(needle, from)` - the search this file said it did not have.

   It is here now because the ASCII scanning family is (Implementation-Vector.md §9 item 8), and that
   family's JS half is the host's own search by ruling rather than by convenience:
   Implementation-String.md part 5 says lowering it to `String.prototype.indexOf` is "legitimate,
   encouraged", on the grounds that searching for one fixed unit has no room for cross-engine
   disagreement the way the grapheme tables do. What it replaces is a `charCodeAt` loop, which clears
   the §7.2 floor and still loses to the engine's own C++.

   `-1` for "not found" is the host's answer and is what the caller turns into a `Nothing`. Not
   wrapped here: this file's whole job is to hand over what the host does, and a `Maybe` is a
   decision the library above it makes.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostIndexOf(self: String, needle: String, from: Size) -> Size
    = hostCall2(self, "indexOf", needle, from)

{-
   `self.lastIndexOf(needle, from)` - the same search from the other end, and the same `-1`.

   `from` is where the match may *start* at the latest rather than where the scan begins, which is
   the host's own definition and is what a caller wanting the whole string passes `length(self)` for.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostLastIndexOf(self: String, needle: String, from: Size) -> Size
    = hostCall2(self, "lastIndexOf", needle, from)

{-
   `self.substring(from, to)` - the units between two positions, as a string of its own.

   Implementation-String.md part 4's slice on this target, and the reason the library's `String.slice`
   is not a `charCodeAt` loop building a string one unit at a time: that loop is a concatenation per
   unit, so cutting one line out of a megabyte of text walked the whole rope for every line.

   The host clamps both bounds into `[0, length]` and swaps them if they arrive the wrong way round.
   `String.slice` clamps first anyway, because the native half has to and the two must answer the
   same thing.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostSubstring(self: String, from: Size, to: Size) -> String
    = hostCall2(self, "substring", from, to)

{-
   Concatenation and comparison, as the host's own operators - see NativeOp::HostBinary.

   `<` on two host strings is raw UTF-16 code-unit order, which is exactly the default `Ord(String)`
   part 3 specifies, wrinkle included: it can disagree with true code-point order for
   supplementary-plane characters, and part 3 says so and points anyone who needs otherwise at an
   explicit `compareByCodePoint`. Getting that for free is the argument for the operator node.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostConcat(self: String, other: String) -> String = hostBinary(self, "+", other)
@inline(always) pub fn hostStringEq(self: String, other: String) -> Bool = hostBinary(self, "===", other)
@inline(always) pub fn hostStringLt(self: String, other: String) -> Bool = hostBinary(self, "<", other)

-- `String.fromCharCode(unit)` - one UTF-16 unit as a one-unit string, which is what appending a unit
-- to a host string has to go through. A call on the host's global rather than on a value, which is
-- why it is the one `HostGlobalCall` here.
@inline(always) pub fn hostFromCharCode(unit: Int) -> String
    = hostGlobal1("String.fromCharCode", unit)

-- `console.log(text)` - the host's own output, which is what `print` is there. A call on a global
-- rather than on a value, like `String.fromCharCode` above.
@inline(always) pub fn hostLog(text: String) -> {} = hostGlobal1("console.log", text) :: {}

{-
   The float text tier - Analysis-Library.md §2.1's JS column.

   The other target computes all of this itself, out of Ryu and its tables. Here it is the host's
   own, and that is a ruling rather than a shortcut: what each of these produces is specified exactly
   - `toExponential(n)` is *the nearest decimal with `n` digits after the point*, `Number` is a
   correctly rounded decimal-to-double conversion, and `String` below is the shortest round trip - so
   there is no room for the cross-engine disagreement that keeps the grapheme tables out of the host.
   Two engines that disagree about any of them are two engines one of which is wrong.

   Reading the digits back out of a string looks like a detour and is not: the alternative is the
   64-bit `Double`/`I64` bitcast this target deliberately does not generate (see
   `defineBitcastLadder`, which argues why), and every operation Ryu is written in would then be
   `bigint` arithmetic behind it.

   **The no-argument form is deliberately not declared.** `toExponential()` is a second specified
   shortest form, and its tie rule is the one that disagrees with Ryu's - so a build that reached for
   it had to detect the disagreement and undo it, which is ninety lines that `String` below does not
   need. A declaration nobody may correctly call is worse than no declaration; `Core/Float/Ryu.js.yana`
   is where the argument is written out. What is left here is the *`n` digits* form, which the `Float`
   instance steps upwards through to find the shortest representation at its own width - a number here
   is a double, so the host's own shortest is the shortest for the wrong type.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostToExponentialAt(value: Double, digits: Int) -> String
    = hostCall1(value, "toExponential", digits)

-- `Number(text)` - the host's decimal-to-double conversion. `NaN` for text that is not a number,
-- which is the host's answer and is what the caller turns into an error; the library above decides
-- what a failure is, exactly as it does for `indexOf`'s `-1`.
@inline(always) pub fn hostToNumber(text: String) -> Double = hostGlobal1("Number", text)

{-
   `String(value)` - the inverse of the one above, and the *other* specified shortest form.

   Generic over what it is handed, because the host's own is: a `bigint` and an integral `number`
   both write their digits, which is what makes `Show(Int)` and `Show(Long)` one call here instead of
   a concatenation per digit, and a fractional `number` writes **the shortest decimal that reads back
   as it, with a tie broken to the even digit** - Number::toString, and word for word the rule Ryu
   follows on the other target.

   That last sentence is why this declaration exists at all. `toExponential()` above is shortest too
   and breaks a tie *upwards*, so a JS build using it had to detect the disagreement and undo it -
   two functions, one of which asked the host for a hundred and one digits to decide whether an
   expansion terminated. Asking for the form whose rule already matches deletes all of that; see
   `Core/Float/Ryu.js.yana`, which is one line long where it was ninety.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostToString(value: a) -> String = hostGlobal1("String", value)

{-
   `Date.now()` - the host's clock, and the only one this library asks for.

   A call on a global rather than on a value, like `String.fromCharCode` and `console.log` above.
   Integral milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, as a `number`, which is what the specification says
   this returns and is therefore the same fact on every host - the test `hostIndexOf` and the two
   float conversions are held to.

   **`performance.now()` is deliberately not declared beside it**, and `Native/Clock.js.yana` is
   where the argument is written out: it is absent from a bare script context, its resolution is
   clamped by the engine as a side-channel mitigation, and its origin is per-document. A host
   operation this library cannot rely on being there is one no program above may correctly call.

   A `Double` rather than a `Long`, because a `number` is what arrives: turning it into the target's
   `bigint` is a conversion, and where that conversion belongs is the caller, who knows which unit
   it wanted and therefore which side of it stays exact.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostDateNow() -> Double = hostGlobal0("Date.now")

{-
   `throw "..."` - how a program stops here, and the one thing every host agrees means "stop".

   There is no `abort` in JavaScript and no exit status a script can set; an exception nobody catches
   ends the program and reports what it carried, which is exactly what a failed check needs to do.

   No argument, and the message is the emitter's, and that is a decision now rather than a
   constraint: `Show` and the format primitive are reachable from here since the prelude collapsed
   (Analysis-Modules.md §2.4), and a `checkFailed` that formatted would put the formatting machinery
   in the binary of a program that never printed. What says something is `assert`, which is where a
   program states an invariant.
-}
@inline(always) pub fn hostFail() -> {} = hostThrow("yana: a runtime check failed")
